# Worked examples

Realistic documents rather than isolated expressions: each section is the sort
of thing someone would actually open the app to work out. Every `=>` is a test.

The numeric answers here were computed independently, with Python's exact
`Fraction` arithmetic rounded the same way, rather than copied from this engine.
Worth noting that the float version of the same check disagreed in the fourth
decimal on the mortgage total: the engine keeps rationals exact until display,
so it is the float that drifts.


## A road trip

    distance   = 420 miles
    efficiency = 30 miles/gallon
    fuel price = 3.45 $/gallon

    fuel needed = distance / efficiency => 14 gallon
    trip cost   = fuel needed * fuel price => $48.3

Halving the efficiency doubles the cost, which is the point of writing it this
way rather than typing the number into a calculator.

    efficiency = 15 miles/gallon
    trip cost => $96.6


## A mortgage

    price        = $425,000
    down rate    = 20%
    down payment = down rate * price => $85,000
    financed     = price - down payment => $340,000

    annual rate = 5.5%/year
    term        = 30 year

    payments      = term * 12/year        => 360
    monthly rate  = annual rate/(12/year) => 0.0046

The standard amortisation formula, written out.

    monthly payment
      = monthly rate/(1 - (1 + monthly rate)^(-payments)) * financed
      => $1,930.4826

Total paid over the life of the loan, and how much of it is interest:

    total paid = monthly payment * payments => $694,973.7376
    interest   = total paid - financed      => $354,973.7376


## Compound interest

    principal = 5000
    rate      = 4.3%
    years     = 7

    balance = principal * (1 + rate)^years => 6,713.6727

Solving the same relationship backwards — how long to reach a target — is not
something the engine can isolate, since `years` sits in an exponent. It reports
how far it got instead of failing.

    doubling = principal * (1 + rate)^years == 10,000


## Projectile motion

    height(t) = 1/2*g*t^2 + v0*t + h0

    g  = -9.81 m/s^2
    v0 = 22 m/s
    h0 = 1.5 m

    height(1s) => 18.595 m
    height(2s) => 25.88 m
    height(3s) => 23.355 m

The vertical velocity is the derivative, and it hits zero at the apex.

    velocity = der(height, t) => -9.81 m*t/s^2 + 22 m/s

Feeding it a bare number instead of a time leaves the units visibly
inconsistent, which is the whole reason for carrying them:

    height(1) => 1.5 m + 22 m/s - 4.905 m/s^2


## Resistors in parallel

    R1 = 220 Ω
    R2 = 330 Ω

    parallel = 1/(1/R1 + 1/R2) => 132Ω

Adding a third in parallel drops it further.

    R3 = 1000 Ω
    three up = 1/(1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3) => 116.6078Ω


## How long is that download

    file size = 4.7 GB
    link rate = 25 Mbit/s

    transfer time = file size / link rate in s => 1,504 s
    transfer time in minutes                  => 25.0667 minutes

Note the factor of eight: `GB` is bytes and `Mbit` is bits, and the unit system
keeps that straight without being told.


## Scaling a recipe

    flour per cup = 125 g

    flour = 350 g
    cups  = flour / flour per cup => 2.8

The grams cancel, so the answer is a plain count of cups rather than a
quantity carrying a unit.

    servings = 4
    wanted   = 6

    scale = wanted / servings => 1.5
    flour for six = flour * scale in g => 525 g


## Painting a room

    wall width  = 12 ft
    wall height = 9 ft
    walls       = 4

    area = wall width * wall height * walls => 432 ft^2

    coverage = 350 ft^2/gallon
    coats    = 2

    paint = area * coats / coverage => 2.4686 gallon


## Body mass index

    weight = 82 kg
    height = 1.78 m

    bmi = weight / height^2 => 25.8806 kg/m^2

The same document works in imperial units, because the conversion happens
inside the definition rather than in the numbers.

    weight = 180 lbs
    height = 5 ft + 11 inch

    bmi in kg/m^2 => 25.1046 kg/m^2


## Two equations, two unknowns

Written as a matrix equation and solved by inversion.

    coefficients = [3, 2; 1, -1]
    constants    = [16; 2]

    coefficients * unknowns = constants

    unknowns => [4; 2]

Checking the answer by substitution:

    3*4 + 2*2 => 16
    4 - 2     => 2


## A weekly budget

    weekly spend +=

      groceries = 145
      transport = 40
      coffee    = 22
      other     = 60

    weekly spend => 267

    annual spend = weekly spend * 52 => 13,884
    daily spend  = weekly spend / 7  => 38.1429
